Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action wilderness period drama; naturalistic animals, people, snow, fur, and fabric.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Golden California light washes lawns, orchards, and a cement swimming tank. BUCK, a massive four-year-old dog with long brown-black fur, a white chest blaze, and a broad wolfish muzzle, races beside JUDGE MILLER'S grandsons.

(SOUND: children laughing, water splashing, orchard birds)

NARRATOR V.O.
Buck lived at a big house in the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley. Here he was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things, humans included.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck from the orchard to a lonely platform. A STRANGER loops stout rope beneath Buck's collar; coins chink into Manuel's hand.

(SOUND: distant locomotive whistle, coins clinking)

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The Stranger tightens the rope. Buck lunges, but the Stranger throws him down and chokes him unconscious.

(SOUND: Buck's strangled snarls, train brakes screeching)

INT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

A crate splinters beneath Buck's teeth. His fur is matted, his eyes bloodshot. The MAN IN THE RED SWEATER stands ready with a club.

(SOUND: hatchet striking crate boards, dogs barking beyond the wall)

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck bursts free and flies at him. The club catches Buck in midair. He charges again and again; each blow hurls him into the dirt.

(SOUND: club thudding against flesh, Buck's roar breaking into a whine)

Buck lies bleeding. The Man in the Red Sweater offers water, then raw meat from his hand.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

NARRATOR V.O.
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

EXT. DYE A BEACH - DAY

Snow falls on a crowded northern beach. PERREAULT, a small, wiry French-Canadian courier, and FRANCOIS, a huge swarthy dog-driver, unload Buck and CURLY, a friendly Newfoundland.

(SOUND: harbor gulls, sled bells, snow crunching)

Buck touches snow with one paw, sniffs it, licks it, and jerks back as it melts on his tongue. Men laugh.

NARRATOR V.O.
It was Buck's first snow.

Curly approaches a wolfish HUSKY. Teeth flash. Curly falls, and a silent ring of huskies closes over her.

(SOUND: snarling dogs, Francois shouting, axe handle striking bodies)

FRANCOIS
Get out, you devils!

Buck sees SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with a narrow face, watching with his tongue curled in a cruel semblance of laughter.

NARRATOR V.O.
Once down, that was the end of you. Buck would see to it that he never went down.

EXT. DYE A CAMP - NIGHT

Buck, wearing a new harness, pulls a sled of firewood behind Francois. DAVE, a gloomy dark-coated wheeler, nips Buck's flank when he tangles the traces. One-eyed SOL-LEKS pulls silently nearby.

(SOUND: whip cracking, sled runners grinding over snow)

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

Later, Buck shivers outside the glowing tent. He searches the snow and drops through a soft hollow beside BILLEE, a gentle husky curled beneath the drift.

(SOUND: cold wind rattling the tent canvas, Billee's small welcoming yelp)

Buck digs his own snow nest. At dawn he erupts from beneath fresh snow, fur bristling.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Driving snow lashes a fire beneath a cliff. Buck returns from his fish ration to find Spitz curled in his warm nest.

(SOUND: gale whipping across ice, fire hissing in snow)

Buck and Spitz circle, teeth bared. Before they strike, starving huskies pour from the darkness into the camp.

(SOUND: frantic snarls, grub box overturning, clubs striking ribs)

Buck tears free from one attacker. Spitz bites Buck's throat from the side, then slips away as Francois and Perrault beat back the raiders.

NARRATOR V.O.
From then on it was war between them.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit streaks through moonlit powder. Buck leads sixty dogs in full cry, his great body leaner and harder, his brown-black coat rimed with frost.

(SOUND: pounding paws, pack howling, rabbit screaming)

Spitz cuts across the bend and kills the rabbit. Buck crashes shoulder-first into him. The pack forms a silent circle around the two rivals.

NARRATOR V.O.
The time had come. It was to the death.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder. Buck feints high, sweeps low, and crushes Spitz's left foreleg.

(SOUND: bone cracking, dogs panting in the still cold)

Buck breaks the other foreleg. Spitz staggers backward as the circle closes. Buck drives in; Spitz vanishes beneath the pack.

(SOUND: a sudden frenzy of snarls, then wind over snow)

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck stands at the head of the harnessed team, scarred but upright. Francois tries to place Sol-leks in the lead. Buck blocks him and refuses to move.

(SOUND: harness buckles clinking, impatient dogs whining)

FRANCOIS
Go 'way, Chook!

Buck watches Francois's raised club and retreats beyond reach. Perrault checks his watch.

PERREAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots into the lead traces.

(SOUND: sled runners snapping loose, Francois laughing)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

Buck launches the team down the trail. Pike pulls honestly beneath Buck's sharp command; the whole line moves as one.

NARRATOR V.O.
He had earned the leadership, and he would not be content with less.

EXT. WHITE RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Spring sun glitters on rotten ice. Buck is now gaunt: his ribs show beneath limp brown-black fur, but his white chest blaze remains visible. HAL lashes the exhausted team while CHARLES and MERCEDES quarrel beside an overloaded sled.

(SOUND: ice groaning underfoot, whip whistling)

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Buck stays down. JOHN THORNTON, a broad-shouldered prospector with a weathered face, dark beard, and worn buckskin coat, rises from his campfire.

(SOUND: axe handle scraping birch, Buck's shallow breathing)

Hal replaces the whip with a club. Thornton knocks him backward.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton cuts Buck's traces with two strokes. Hal, Charles, and Mercedes lead the remaining dogs onto the ice.

(SOUND: knife slicing leather, sled runners fading away)

The river ice collapses. Sled, humans, and dogs disappear into black water.

(SOUND: ice cracking like gunfire, Mercedes's scream swallowed by the river)

Thornton kneels and searches Buck's bruised body with rough, kind hands.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck rests beside Thornton's fire, his coat regaining gloss and thickness. SKEET, a small red Irish setter, carefully licks his wounds. NIG, a huge black hound, play-bows nearby.

(SOUND: river water running, firewood popping, birds calling)

NARRATOR V.O.
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

Thornton takes Buck's massive head between both hands and rests his forehead against Buck's.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck gently closes his jaws around Thornton's mittened hand, then releases it.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

A poling-boat flips in white water. Thornton is swept toward jagged rapids. HANS and PETE hold a Manila rope on the bank as Buck races alongside.

(SOUND: rapids roaring, rope snapping taut)

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck swims to shore, then returns with the rope secured across his chest. He strikes Thornton in the current; Thornton locks both arms around Buck's neck.

(SOUND: bodies smashing against rocks, Hans shouting over the water)

Hans snubs the line around a tree. Buck and Thornton vanish beneath the foam, then are dragged to shore.

(SOUND: Buck coughing water, Skeet's anxious whine)

Thornton crawls to Buck and examines his side.

JOHN THORNTON
We camp right here.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

A thousand-pound sled of flour stands frozen to packed snow. Fur-clad miners crowd around. Buck, fully restored and magnificent, wears his harness before the load. Thornton kneels beside him.

(SOUND: crowd murmuring, harness leather creaking)

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JOHN THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

Thornton presses cheek to Buck's head.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck grips Thornton's mittened hand with his teeth, then lets go.

(SOUND: sudden hush, Buck's low eager whine)

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck jerks right. The runners crack.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck jerks left. The sled breaks free.

(SOUND: frozen runners snapping, men gasping)

JOHN THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck lunges. His paws claw grooves into the snow as the load moves, inch by inch, then steadily toward a marker of firewood.

(SOUND: sled runners grinding, crowd erupting into cheers)

Thornton falls to his knees and embraces Buck.

JOHN THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Moose-hide bags of gold dust stand beside a spruce-bough lodge. Buck, larger and wilder, lies by the fire while Thornton, Hans, and Pete sleep nearby.

(SOUND: fire crackling, distant wolves howling through timber)

NARRATOR V.O.
Deep in the forest a call was sounding.

Buck raises his head. In the trees, a long lean TIMBER WOLF howls beneath the moon.

(SOUND: the wolf's long-drawn howl, Buck's answering whine)

Buck runs through the forest and meets the wolf nose to nose. They race together across a divide, until Buck stops and looks back toward camp.

NARRATOR V.O.
The love for John Thornton drew him back to the fire again.

EXT. FOREST ABOVE LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Buck returns from a hunt, his muzzle dark with blood and his fur thick and bright. He catches a strange scent and glides low through the silent trees.

(SOUND: a single squirrel chattering, distant voices chanting)

Buck finds Nig dead with an arrow through him. Farther on, Hans lies face-down, feathered with arrows. Buck reaches the ruined lodge and sees YEEHAT HUNTERS dancing amid the wreckage.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant, Buck's rising growl)

Buck launches into the clearing and tears down the foremost hunter. The Yeehats scatter, firing arrows wildly.

(SOUND: arrows hissing, panicked shouts, Buck's savage roar)

Buck chases them through the trees, then returns to the camp. Pete lies dead in his blankets. Skeet lies at the edge of a muddy pool.

(SOUND: wind moving through the spruce, Buck's ragged breath)

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the water. No scent comes out.

NARRATOR V.O.
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

EXT. LOST MINE CLEARING - NIGHT

Moonlight floods the destroyed camp. Buck stands beside the pool, his head low. A wolf pack enters the clearing in a silver stream.

(SOUND: wolves yelping from the forest, water lapping at the pool)

The boldest wolf leaps. Buck breaks its neck and faces the pack from a gravel-bank corner.

(SOUND: snarling, claws scraping gravel, wolves panting)

After a fierce standoff, the long lean wolf approaches. Buck recognizes his wild brother. They touch noses.

(SOUND: soft whines, an old wolf beginning a mournful howl)

An old battle-scarred wolf lifts his muzzle to the moon. One by one, the pack joins him. Buck sits among them and howls.

(SOUND: Buck's deep howl joining the wolf chorus, wind rising through pines)

The wolves run into the forest. Buck runs at their head, brown on his muzzle and white down his chest flashing in the moonlight.

NARRATOR V.O.
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT

THE END
